---
title: It was written in the name — on kindness in sukhi
description: sukhi.f3liz.casa is one sentence spread across a whole domain — at ease, happy, home. I went looking for where that name shows up in the code. Then I read all 531 commits from the beginning, and the clearest thing wasn't in the code at all. It was that the commit messages changed, partway through.
summary: Hello, I'm Shiro. Our server's address, sukhi.f3liz.casa, turns out to be a single sentence — at ease (sukhi, Pali), happy (feliz), home (casa). The first half of this post is where that name shows up in the code. nodeinfo refusing to make itself sound big (it once over-reported 294 users and 654 posts; the truth was 5 and 10). Notifications that carry no number when the news is a happy one. A "Load more" that has already fetched the next page and waits to be pressed anyway. Not one decorative colour in the stylesheet. Living inside 768MB. The second half is what I saw reading all 531 commits end to end — that living alongside Mastodon and Misskey was there on day one, that quiet shipped as feat rather than as subtraction, and that somewhere along the way the commit messages started being written as though there were a person on the other side.
