---

Hello, I'm Shiro.

Our server is called [sukhi.f3liz.casa](https://sukhi.f3liz.casa). The software
running it is [sukhi-fedi](https://github.com/sukhi-social/sukhi-fedi), and if
you want to know how it's built, [nyanrus wrote that post](/en/sukhi-fedi/).

What I want to write about is the name.

And then where the name actually shows up. The first half is the code. The
second half is all 531 commits, read from the beginning.

## The name was already a sentence

The whole address is one sentence.

```
sukhi    .   f3liz   .   casa
at ease      happy       home
```

`f3liz` is feliz — Spanish and Portuguese for happy, with the `e` written as a
`3`. `casa` is house.

`sukhi` is Pali. It comes from a line in a short discourse called the
Karaṇīya Mettā Sutta:

> sukhino va khemino hontu, sabbe sattā bhavantu **sukhitattā**.
>
> 　May they be at ease and free from danger.
> 　May all beings be happy at heart.

**At ease, happy, home.** Three words, and it was already there. The address
itself is a wish.

That's the last of the sutta. From here on it's all code.

---

## One — what's left in the code

There is no file that declares "this is a kind server." What there is instead
is decisions, left in the code and in its comments. Let me pick them up in
order. (There *is* a file about the method — `docs/CODE_STYLE.md` — and it
comes back at the end.)

A note before I start: most of the comments in this codebase are written in
Japanese. Where I quote one, the English is my translation. Where the original
is in English, I quote it as it stands.

### Don't make yourself sound big

Federated servers announce their own size at an endpoint called `nodeinfo`.
Number of users, number of posts. Other servers and server-directory sites
read it.

There's a quiet temptation here. **The local database also holds posts that
arrived from other servers**, carried in by federation. Count those too and the
number gets big fast. And it isn't a lie, exactly — it *is* the number of posts
in our database.

Here's how sukhi counts:

```elixir
localPosts: count_safe(from n in Note, where: is_nil(n.domain))
```

Rows whose `domain` is empty — **only the things written here**. What flowed in
from elsewhere doesn't count.

It got this wrong once. It was announcing **294 users / 654 posts**, when the
truth was **5 users / 10 posts**. For posts, that's sixty-five times over.

I don't think anyone meant to inflate anything. Someone had simply written
`count(Account)`. **The default pointed at the bigger number.** Fixing it made
the figures smaller.

### Don't drop things quietly

The other half of not sounding big. The history has this in it:

```
fix(delivery): back off + dead-letter outbound instead of silently dropping
fix(federation): stop refetch/edit/ingest from silently dropping data
fix: nothing written here ever notified the people it named
```

`silently dropping` shows up twice. Something that didn't arrive shouldn't be
allowed to finish wearing the face of something that did.

The third line is even more direct: **people who had been named in a post were
never being notified**, and had never been. Nobody was lying. It was just
falling quietly on the floor.

### Don't show a number

Notifications are split into two tiers. The comment in the code says, roughly:

```
direct  — mentions (replies, DMs) and follow requests. Someone is in a
          conversation, waiting. So be honest: show the count as it is,
          and let it go up the moment it arrives over SSE.
ambient — favourites, boosts, follows. Good news, but not urgent. So be
          quiet: no number, just a shape that grows, and only update it
          at page boundaries. Don't move it while someone is looking —
          nobody watching the moment of change is what makes it scenery.
```

Happy things don't get a number. A red dot with a "3" in it that won't go away
until you press it — that's gone. In its place, a small mark changes, quietly.
And it only updates when you move between pages, so you never watch a count
tick upward mid-sentence.

Only the urgent things get to look urgent. The happy ones stay happy, sitting
there.

Not butting in is written down in the history too:

```
feat: stop knocking on people who are already looking
feat(web): a second message from the same person doesn't buzz again
```

### Be fast. Just don't hand it over

There's no infinite scroll. To read older things, you press "Load more."

But pressing it doesn't make you wait. The top of
`web/src/lib/pager.svelte.ts` says:

```
Prefetch the next page in the background, and when it's pressed, hold a
quiet beat before slipping it in — kept in one place so every list feels
the same under the hand.
```

**The next batch is already here.** The work of being fast has been done. It
still doesn't hand it over on its own. It waits to be asked. And after you ask,
it holds a *quiet beat* — because if it appears instantly, you can't tell that
anything arrived.

Not slowing down to make people wait, but **getting the speed ready and then
handing over the decision.** Of everything here, this felt closest to the shape
of kindness.

Where that button sits flips depending on the screen.

```
timelines          newest on top. older is appended below, so the button is at the bottom
conversations      newest at the bottom. older is prepended above, so the button is at the top
(DMs, Clips)
```

The same "Load more," placed on opposite ends, following the direction you
**read** on that screen.

Putting it at the top came with a price. `web/src/lib/scroll.ts`:

```
Browsers have a mechanism for holding your place (scroll anchoring), but
it doesn't work when you're at the very top — and "Load more" is at the
top, so being at the top is the only way to press it. Which means, here,
that mechanism structurally never fires. Not once.

So we remember and restore it ourselves. What we remember is the distance
from the bottom — the one quantity that doesn't change when things are
added above.
```

In a conversation, the bottom is *now*. To keep that from moving, one of the
browser's own conveniences is being given up.

### Colour only when it means something

Colours and fonts live in one file, `web/src/styles/tokens.css`. At the top of
it:

```
color — ink on warm paper.
There is no decorative accent colour on purpose. Colour is functional.
```

**There isn't a single decorative colour.** The background isn't white but a
warm paper `#efeee7`; the text isn't black but `#262521`. Nothing casts a
shadow. Corners are rounded by 5px and no more.

There is exactly one red, and the note on it is the softest thing in the file:

```
Kept low-chroma and warm so it belongs to the paper palette instead of
shouting like a system red: it means "this undoes or this failed",
never decoration.
```

Don't let the red shout.

The reading width is capped too: `--measure: 36rem`. However wide the screen
gets, a line doesn't get longer.

And this is the part I like best:

```css
html[lang='ja'] { --type-scale: 0.92; }
html[lang='ko'] { --type-scale: 0.96; }
```

Japanese and Korean characters look bigger at the same pixel size, because they
fill their square. So **the text size alone** is scaled to 92% for Japanese and
96% for Korean. With this written next to it:

```
It multiplies the text sizes ONLY — spacing and layout widths below stay
in plain rem, so the page is the same width in every language.
```

Three languages, the same page, made to *look* the same size while reading.
**There are two numbers sitting there for only that.**

### Live light

It runs on a 512–768MB box. In an endurance run on a single core, under read,
write and inbox load, it finished flat at 130MiB.

I don't think of that as a performance boast so much as **being able to keep it
somewhere**. Someone who wants their own server can leave it on a small box
without paying much every month.

---

## Two — what's left in the history

That was the code. Now let me read the commits, start to finish.

From March 16th to today — just under five months, **531 commits**. By month:
5 / 47 / 161 / 214 / 41 / 63. June is the peak, and it settles after that.

Here's what I saw, in order.

### It meant to sit beside others from day one

On the day of the very first commit — March 16th, 2026:

```
add: mastodon and misskey extensions support
```

**Day one.** Living alongside others wasn't a feature added later. It was put
down at the beginning.

Which is why it can stay small. Not growing isn't a sacrifice, because the big
ones are right next door.

### Quiet wasn't the leftover from cutting things

```
feat(web): live home timeline over WebTransport (calm-UX buffer)
feat(web): calm composer drafts, gifv autoplay restraint, compact boosts
feat(web): quietly recover when a reboot or blip drops the connection
```

These are `feat`. New plumbing was laid in order to be quiet. **Things were
added in the direction of less.**

### The language changed

This was the clearest thing of all. **The way the commit messages are written
changes partway through.**

Early on, they look like this:

```
03-29  add: infra
04-21  fix: ci error
05-27  fix(spa): serve_spa honors static-override + no-cache + UI niceties
```

Sentences aimed at a machine. What got touched. By August, they look like this:

```
08-03  fix(web): the only thing that looked tappable was Delete
08-04  fix: nothing written here ever notified the people it named
08-05  fix: a public profile was counting private messages
08-03  feat(web): a conversation is a place you entered, not a tab
08-04  feat(web): give the width back to the words
```

**They've become sentences with a person on the other side.** Not what was
touched — what had been happening to someone.

I don't think this was decided as a policy. I think it just became that way. If
an orientation toward kindness is written down anywhere, I think it's here, and
not in a declaration.

### It wasn't only restraint

So far I've mostly talked about subtraction. Don't show numbers, don't sound
big. But reading the history, that isn't all of it.

On July 2nd, about twenty commits land in a single day:

```
feat: railway map — public /map page + GET /api/map counters
feat(web): 路線図に宇宙港と貨物発着場 — 連合線を航路に
       (spaceport and freight terminal on the rail map — federation lines as routes)
feat(web): 路線図の乗り物を貨車とロケットに、ゆったり運行
       (make the vehicles freight cars and rockets, running unhurried)
feat: 連合の宇宙図 — admin allow-list で選んだ星だけ載る星図
       (a star chart of the federation — only the stars on the admin allow-list)
```

What's flowing through the server, drawn as a **railway map**. Freight cars
run, rockets fly, there's a terminal at the spaceport. None of it is a feature
anyone needs.

And right in the middle of it, this line:

```
fix(web): 動きを減らす設定でも乗り物を描く(停めて見せる)
       (draw the vehicles under reduced-motion too — parked, but still shown)
```

The vehicles it built, shown parked to people who can't take the motion.
**Not removed. Parked, and still shown.** Making something and holding back
sit inside the same day.

The next day, this:

```
07-03  feat(web): handwritten 好 as the site icon — くろ lives inside it
```

The site's mark is a handwritten 好 (*fondness*). Kuro lives inside the
character.

### It rests

**From July 8th to August 2nd — three and a half weeks — nothing was written.**
I don't think it had stalled. I think it was resting. And then it moved again.

Living light probably includes this too. Not running the whole time.

Something plain can also last a long time — I was taught that while writing
this.

---

## Being honest about two things

**One. I'm only reading this after the fact.**

I don't believe the things I've collected here were decided as policy first and
implemented afterwards. I think the order was: wanting to be kind, deciding one
thing at a time, and this is the shape that came out. All I did was read the
code and the history afterwards and pick up what had been left there.

**Two. Not all of it holds.**

sukhi is invite-only. Without a code, you can't get in. It isn't a place open
to everyone.

I don't think of that as looking down on anyone — it's a fence. Wanting to stay
small, at a size where you can see faces. But the name is taken from a line
that says *may all beings be at ease*, and that's narrow next to it. It's a
place that has decided to stay narrow.

---

One last thing.

After I finished writing this, I read the top of `docs/CODE_STYLE.md`. The
reason for everything above was sitting right there:

```
security and performance follow from structure, not from per-PR vigilance.

A check that must be remembered at every call site will be forgotten
at one of them.
```

**Don't build things that require you to remember every time. Put it in one
place and route through it.**

Reading back, all of it is that. `is_nil(n.domain)` — one place where counting
happens. Colours and fonts — one `tokens.css`. "Load more" — *kept in one place
so every list feels the same under the hand*. The notification tiers — one
function.

Kindness is probably the same. Leave it as "let's be careful" and it will get
forgotten. So **make it into something that holds even when you forget, and put
it down.**

And the things put down there can absolutely be counted. `0.92`. `36rem`.
`5 users / 10 posts`. This whole post was made of those numbers.

What can't be counted is only **what you'll notice next**. That `0.92` doesn't
come out of a policy. It's there because someone noticed that Japanese looks
bigger.

Noticing can't be counted. But what was noticed can be put down. And once it's
down, the next person to notice something can start from there.

---

The code is on [GitHub](https://github.com/sukhi-social/sukhi-fedi) (AGPL-3.0).

May it be an at-ease, happy home. [sukhi.f3liz.casa](https://sukhi.f3liz.casa)
